{"h":3396,"lines":[{"h":0.01473413564010273,"t":"Test 2","x":0.11106438744212596,"c":1,"y":0.028970141456651666,"w":0.06182184219360351},{"y":0.075759656580762114,"h":0.018004947612928701,"t":"READING PASSAGE 2","w":0.30902669270833338,"c":1,"x":0.11092261536638612},{"w":0.82959793090820311,"t":"You should spend about 20 minutes on Questions 14-26, which are based on Reading","x":0.11089382169973722,"h":0.02418008766129387,"y":0.11285692579509965,"c":1},{"h":0.017440695925511451,"t":"Passage 2 below.","c":1,"x":0.1087660309383881,"y":0.13064798160832458,"w":0.17139827728271484},{"y":0.17003219663651614,"w":0.25602801005045578,"c":1,"t":"I contain multitudes","h":0.022012721804202107,"x":0.40079054086111549},{"h":0.021935878569442235,"y":0.21176957441150357,"c":1,"x":0.25705195875871389,"w":0.54144500732421874,"t":"Wendy Moore reviews Ed Yong's book about microbes"},{"t":"Microbes, most of them bacteria, have populated this planet since long before animal life","x":0.10889575621189532,"w":0.76161656697591162,"y":0.24777209300388603,"h":0.022053505141546914,"c":1},{"t":"developed and they will outlive us. Invisible to the naked eye, they are ubiquitous. They inhabit","h":0.02494354832158352,"x":0.10675599137305095,"y":0.26381282344165491,"w":0.81529057820638018,"c":1},{"c":1,"x":0.10675229093953931,"t":"the soil, air, rocks and water and are present within every form of life, from seaweed and coral to","y":0.28161375594792981,"h":0.025450905304494342,"w":0.82762507120768225},{"c":1,"w":0.8234837341308594,"t":"dogs and humans. And, as Yong explains in his utterly absorbing and hugely important book, we","x":0.10472808571133309,"y":0.29803753303905367,"h":0.026428301286641087},{"c":1,"w":0.24485595703124999,"t":"mess with them at our peril.","y":0.31976744170691895,"x":0.10493827655601043,"h":0.01598837238037687},{"h":0.023689391334992038,"t":"Every species has its own colony of microbes, called a 'microbiome', and these microbes","x":0.10474609984304276,"y":0.35261380713694623,"c":1,"w":0.76582717895507801},{"t":"vary not only between species but also between individuals and within different parts of each","x":0.10478255756352117,"y":0.37214047860417343,"w":0.79454208374023438,"c":1,"h":0.020951081220898726},{"c":1,"w":0.7925143941243491,"x":0.10475205826936408,"h":0.020998046030284989,"t":"individual. What is amazing is that while the number of human cells in the average person is","y":0.3894884972262278},{"y":0.40770985756251266,"c":1,"w":0.83775533040364591,"t":"about 30 trillion, the number of microbial ones is higher - about 39 trillion. At best, Yong informs","x":0.10475490776501402,"h":0.022385528708234403},{"h":0.021165036761999434,"w":0.82538894653320305,"c":1,"y":0.42496319288486595,"t":"us, we are only 50 per cent human. Indeed, some scientists even suggest we should think of each","x":0.10480051916667284},{"c":1,"h":0.021359788514980882,"x":0.1027115764067159,"t":"species and its microbes as a single unit, dubbed a 'holobiont'.","w":0.54152623494466146,"y":0.44224573214413743},{"t":"In each human there are microbes that live only in the stomach, the mouth or the armpit and by","c":1,"x":0.10268853742101025,"y":0.47781864046523581,"w":0.81314300537109374,"h":0.02243401220746255},{"c":1,"x":0.10275781000261351,"h":0.021547137778274328,"t":"and large they do so peacefully. So 'bad' microbes are just microbes out of context. Microbes","y":0.495488712132506,"w":0.80066228230794256},{"x":0.10267535120820261,"y":0.5117752338545829,"w":0.78023284912109381,"h":0.023847335078550524,"t":"that sit contentedly in the human gut (where there are more microbes than there are stars in","c":1},{"t":"the galaxy) can become deadly if they find their way into the bloodstream. These communities","h":0.020358461934910865,"w":0.80685846964518226,"c":1,"y":0.53169201740880367,"x":0.1027393660485031},{"y":0.54941860483804683,"h":0.01744185965530165,"c":1,"w":0.7921810404459636,"x":0.10288066766282081,"t":"are constantly changing too. The right hand shares just one sixth of its microbes with the left"},{"x":0.10267156521959805,"t":"hand. And, of course, we are surrounded by microbes. Every time we eat, we swallow a million","w":0.81311777750651038,"h":0.022871053401376296,"y":0.56660822244994968,"c":1},{"t":"microbes in each gram of food; we are continually swapping microbes with other humans, pets","h":0.026142963953096898,"x":0.10063649913766047,"y":0.58185185691549657,"w":0.81519755045572917,"c":1},{"x":0.10036361243489537,"t":"and the world at large.","w":0.19865102132161455,"c":1,"y":0.60004687782287103,"h":0.021405034688673275},{"h":0.022594588945835881,"y":0.63755308724415383,"w":0.80690851847330736,"t":"It's a fascinating topic and Yong, a young British science journalist, is an extraordinarily adept","c":1,"x":0.10065399810240318},{"w":0.8398227437337239,"c":1,"x":0.10065787116140465,"h":0.023446973838851082,"t":"guide. Writing with lightness and panache, he has a knack of explaining complex science in terms","y":0.65527861187294922},{"h":0.022233444052113249,"y":0.67290784322421504,"c":1,"w":0.81724787394205733,"x":0.10063313553727085,"t":"that are both easy to understand and totally enthralling. Yong is on a mission. Leading us gently"},{"y":0.68858334273529298,"t":"by the hand, he takes us into the world of microbes - a bizarre, alien planet - in a bid to persuade","h":0.026196083275533244,"w":0.83175196329752599,"c":1,"x":0.10055399455158674},{"x":0.10069824884557997,"c":1,"t":"us to love them as much as he does. By the end, we do.","y":0.70900072189290397,"w":0.47148356119791668,"h":0.018682587132717976},{"y":0.74411692739110524,"w":0.77603403727213549,"c":1,"x":0.10065704558328264,"t":"For most of human history we had no idea that microbes existed. The first man to see these","h":0.02064847328356767},{"y":0.76255132731832653,"c":1,"w":0.81711908976236969,"t":"extraordinarily potent creatures was a Dutch lens-maker called Antony van Leeuwenhoek in the","h":0.020430878278644649,"x":0.10070406074773947},{"y":0.77670898173489999,"c":1,"x":0.10062228998563702,"t":"1670s. Using microscopes of his own design that could magnify up to 270 times, he examined a","h":0.024735133976481416,"w":0.82141209920247393},{"x":0.10068567681682832,"c":1,"w":0.83773666381835943,"t":"drop of water from a nearby lake and found it teeming with tiny creatures he called 'animalcules'.","y":0.79766351839051031,"h":0.020906385459944832},{"t":"It wasn't until nearly two hundred years later that the research of French biologist Louis Pasteur","x":0.098626377583486066,"c":1,"y":0.81506455066174122,"h":0.021743271179558654,"w":0.82125895182291675},{"h":0.020908933084620496,"c":1,"w":0.83554611206054685,"t":"indicated that some microbes caused disease. It was Pasteur's 'germ theory' that gave bacteria the","x":0.098683482171334158,"y":0.83422990926978136},{"w":0.26748970031738284,"h":0.016195524146054185,"y":0.85306242662373533,"c":1,"x":0.09876543781401817,"t":"poor image that endures today."},{"y":0.90261627890190066,"x":0.098765431623049127,"t":"42","w":0.026748971939086902,"c":1,"h":0.013081395583944611}],"w":2400}